{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The Lean statement ranges over arbitrary sentence types and functions K and F. It does not require K to be the syntactic operator B or F φ to result from substituting the Gödel numeral of φ into a single one-free-variable formula. Object-language arithmetic and quantification are not represented."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The Lean hypotheses are sufficient conditions extracted from “T includes Q,” not an equivalent rendering of it. 𝓢 need only include the theorems of an abstract classical base, with distribution for K required in 𝓢; neither system is required to contain the specific axioms of Q."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def5 assumes a base-provable fixed point for the chosen F. In the source, availability of that fixed point for every formula F in scope comes from the arithmetical fixed point theorem, not from a separate hypothesis on F."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Def1 requires necessitation for every theorem of the chosen base. This is a more uniform requirement than the proof remark's cited need for Bp for tautologies and the elementary arithmetic and equality axioms used for the fixed point; it does not impose necessitation on 𝓢."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Within the declared abstraction, the case (i) hypothesis and conclusion match exactly: 𝓢 proves K (F φ → φ) for each φ, and for some ψ it proves the conjunction of K ψ and K (¬F ψ). The caveats concern how the arithmetic setting supplies the premises, not the scope of K or the direction of the claim."
}
